{{Japanese name|Taguchi}}

'''[[Toshiyuki Taguchi]]''' ({{nihongo|田口 敏之|Taguchi Toshiyuki}}; 12 June 1839 – 8 November 1892) was a Sanese politician who served as the interim [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from March to August 1881, concurrently holding the office of [[Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal (Okaiken)|Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal]]. An independent politician aligned with the conservative faction of the early [[Saito Restoration]] establishment, Taguchi's brief tenure is generally regarded by historians as a continuation of the preceding [[Shō Konoe|Konoe]] government rather than a distinct administration.

==Early life and education==
[[Toshiyuki Taguchi]] was born in [[Toyozawa]] in 1839, the son of a mid-ranking samurai retainer of the Honda Shogunate. His family belonged to the fudai class of hereditary vassals, and young Toshiyuki was educated in the traditional Confucian curriculum at the Shogunal academy in Toyozawa.

During the final decades of the [[Honda Shogunate]], Taguchi was drawn into the reformist movement that would eventually coalesce into the [[Saito Restoration]]. He was not a revolutionary; like [[Komatsu Sumiyoshi]] and [[Mitsunari Taku]], he was a product of the Shogunal bureaucracy who concluded that the old order could not survive. After the Restoration, he studied law at [[Okami Imperial University]], becoming one of the first generation of Sanese bureaucrats trained in the Volisanian constitutional model. His legal training and connections within the former Shogunal establishment made him a valuable bridge between the old and new orders, and aligned him with the conciliatory, continuity-minded wing of the early Saito establishment.

==Political career==
Taguchi entered government service in the early 1870s as a junior official in the newly created Ministry of Civil Affairs. His administrative competence and his ability to navigate the complex factional politics of the early Saito era earned him rapid promotion. By 1875, he had been appointed to the Privy Council, the advisory body to [[Saito of Mitoyagi|Emperor Saito]].

In 1878, Taguchi was appointed [[Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal (Okaiken)|Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal]], a position that placed him at the center of imperial-state relations. The Lord Keeper was responsible for safeguarding the imperial seal and countersigning state documents, giving Taguchi intimate access to the Emperor and considerable influence over the flow of information between the throne and the cabinet.

==Interim Prime Minister (1881)==
[[File:Early Saito Industrialization 1881.png|thumb|right|200px|Early industrialization efforts during the Saito era]]
Upon the resignation of [[Shō Konoe]]'s government in March 1881, the Emperor faced a political crisis. Konoe's cabinet had collapsed after failing to secure revisions to the unequal treaties imposed by [[Dagit]], and no faction within the ruling establishment could agree on a successor. The Emperor accepted Konoe's resignation but, rather than dissolving the government entirely, invited Taguchi—as Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal—to head the administration on an interim basis.

Taguchi's government lasted only five months, from March to August 1881. During this period, the cabinet continued the policies of the Konoe administration without significant deviation. Taguchi made no major policy initiatives, viewing his role as purely caretaker. He oversaw the ongoing negotiations with [[Dagit]] powers over treaty revision, though these made no progress during his tenure.

The most significant act of Taguchi's interim government was the promulgation of the Dagit Trade Regulation Ordinance, which attempted—unsuccessfully—to impose tariffs on imports from the [[Dagit]] signatory powers. The ordinance was quickly struck down by the Privy Council as inconsistent with the existing treaty framework.

=== Domestic policy ===
Taguchi's interim government was primarily concerned with maintaining governmental continuity rather than initiating new policy. The most significant domestic achievement of the caretaker administration was the continuation of the Saito Restoration's modernization program, ensuring that the momentum of reform was not lost during the political transition. Taguchi oversaw budget stabilization measures, including the reduction of military expenditures that had ballooned under the Konoe government and the consolidation of the national debt. The interim government also maintained the administrative apparatus of the prefectural system established under the first Miyazaki government, appointing reliable bureaucrats to governorships that had been left vacant by the political crisis.

=== Foreign policy ===
In foreign affairs, Taguchi maintained the existing treaty framework without significant modification. The ongoing negotiations with the [[Dagit]] powers over treaty revision continued under the direction of the Foreign Ministry, though no progress was made during the interim period. Taguchi's government also maintained the Shendan policy of the preceding administration, preserving the commercial and diplomatic relationships that had been established with Shendan states. The interim government's most notable foreign policy act was the unsuccessful Dagit Trade Regulation Ordinance, which attempted to impose tariffs on imports from the Dagit signatory powers but was struck down by the Privy Council as inconsistent with the existing treaty framework.

Today, Taguchi's government is generally regarded by historians as a continuation of Konoe's administration rather than a distinct premiership. The official numbering of Sanese prime ministers reflects this view, listing Taguchi as an interim holder rather than a numbered prime minister.

==Later life and death==
Following the appointment of [[Kanezane Enoki]] as prime minister in August 1881, Taguchi returned to his duties as Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal. He continued to serve in this capacity until declining health forced his retirement in 1884.

Taguchi died in [[Okami]] on 8 November 1892 at the age of 53.

==Legacy==
Taguchi's historical significance lies primarily in his role as Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal during the formative years of the Saito state. His brief interim premiership is remembered as an example of the institutional fragility of the early Saito constitutional order, when the absence of clear succession mechanisms forced the Emperor to rely on court officials to maintain governmental continuity.

